---
registryId: kafka
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Apache Kafka?

**Apache Kafka is a distributed, durable log for moving ordered streams of events
between systems.** Producers append records to named topics. Kafka keeps those records
for a configured retention period, and independent consumers read them at their own
pace by tracking offsets.

Kafka matters when several applications need the same event history, when a consumer
must recover by replaying old data, or when one machine cannot sustain the required
throughput. It is more than a task queue: consuming a record does not delete it, and a
second consumer group can read the same history independently.

{% callout variant="info" %}
The core invariant is **ordered, append-only history within one partition**. Kafka does
not provide one global order across a topic. A record key chooses the partition, so key
design chooses the scope of ordering, parallelism, and hotspot risk.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Follow one event from publish to replay

A producer fetches topic metadata, chooses a partition, and sends a batch to that
partition's leader broker. Followers copy the leader's log. A consumer group assigns
each partition to at most one group member at a time, while another group can read the
same partitions for a different purpose.

{% system-flow title="Kafka data path" caption="The partition log is the durable handoff. Producers and consumers can scale independently as long as partition ownership remains explicit." %}
{% system-node title="Producer" eyebrow="Serialize + key" icon="activity" tone="blue" %}
Build a record with a key, value, timestamp, and optional headers. Batch and compress
records before sending them to a partition leader.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Partition leader" eyebrow="Append" icon="database" tone="violet" %}
Assign the next offset and append the batch to one ordered log. The leader serves reads
and coordinates replication for that partition.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Follower replicas" eyebrow="Copy" icon="repeat" tone="cyan" %}
Replicate the partition so another broker can become leader after a failure. The
in-sync replica set determines which copies are sufficiently caught up.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Consumer group" eyebrow="Read + commit" icon="users" tone="green" %}
Poll assigned partitions, process records, and commit offsets that describe completed
progress. Lag is the distance between the latest and committed offsets.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Topic" eyebrow="Named stream" icon="layers" tone="blue" %}
A logical event category such as `orders.v1`. Topic configuration controls partitions,
replication, retention, compaction, and record limits.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Partition" eyebrow="Order + scale" icon="route" tone="violet" %}
One ordered log with offsets `0, 1, 2...`. More partitions allow more broker and
consumer parallelism but do not create global ordering.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Record key" eyebrow="Placement contract" icon="key" tone="amber" %}
Records with the same stable key normally reach the same partition. That preserves
per-key order but can create a hotspot when one key dominates traffic.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Offset" eyebrow="Read position" icon="workflow" tone="green" %}
An offset identifies a record's position inside one partition. Committed offsets are
consumer progress, not proof that an external side effect succeeded exactly once.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Size the topic from workload, not folklore

Start with logical ingress, then account for retention, replication, broker headroom,
partition load, and consumer capacity. The model below is deliberately transparent:
its broker and consumer limits are planning assumptions, not universal Kafka limits.

- **Logical ingress:** `events/sec x average encoded bytes`.
- **Retained replica bytes:** `logical ingress x retention seconds x replication factor`.
- **Active consumers:** the smaller of consumer instances and partition count.
- **Per-consumer demand:** `events/sec / active consumers`.
- **Operational reserve:** keep disk, network, and partition leadership below measured
  saturation so a broker can fail without turning recovery into overload.

{% interactive-block id="technology/kafka-cluster" dataFile="/api/content/technology/kafka/data/topic-capacity-model.json" /%}

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/kafka/code/estimate-topic-capacity.py" language="python" title="Estimate retained bytes and consumer demand" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Partition by the ordering boundary

Partition count is both a capacity decision and an API contract. Increasing it later
can change the key-to-partition mapping for future records, so consumers must not rely
on accidental ordering between unrelated keys.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Name the ordered entity" label="Invariant" %}
Choose the smallest business identity whose events must stay ordered, such as
`order_id`, `account_id`, or `device_id`.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Estimate the hottest key" label="Skew" %}
Average traffic hides celebrity accounts, large tenants, and noisy devices. Measure
the largest key's share and decide how that entity can be isolated or subdivided.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Budget partition load" label="Capacity" %}
Benchmark encoded event size, compression, acknowledgement policy, and storage on the
target hardware. Divide peak load by a conservative per-partition envelope.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Preserve routing evolution" label="Change" %}
Version custom partitioning rules and schema contracts. Treat a partition-count change
as an ordering and replay review, not a harmless scaling toggle.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Avoid the two partitioning extremes

- **Too few partitions** cap broker distribution and consumer-group parallelism.
- **Too many partitions** increase metadata, open files, leader elections, replication
  work, and rebalance coordination.
- **Random or missing keys** spread writes but discard per-entity ordering.
- **One dominant key** preserves order while concentrating traffic on one partition.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Compose durability from acknowledgements and replication

Replication factor says how many broker copies should exist. Producer `acks` says what
must happen before a send completes. `min.insync.replicas` can reject a write when too
few replicas are caught up, converting a possible durability loss into visible
unavailability.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="acks=0" eyebrow="No broker confirmation" icon="speed" tone="rose" %}
The client does not wait for a broker response. This can reduce waiting but cannot tell
the application whether the broker accepted the batch.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="acks=1" eyebrow="Leader confirmation" icon="server" tone="amber" %}
The leader acknowledges after its local append. A leader failure before followers copy
the record can lose an acknowledged write.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="acks=all" eyebrow="In-sync confirmation" icon="shield" tone="green" %}
The leader waits for the required in-sync replicas. Pair this with a meaningful minimum
ISR and enough replicas across independent failure domains.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Producer idempotence prevents broker retries from appending duplicate records within
the producer protocol. It requires compatible acknowledgement and in-flight settings.
It does **not** deduplicate a new application-level send with a new identity, nor does
it make an external database write exactly once.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Trace delivery failures before choosing a guarantee

Terms such as *at least once* and *exactly once* are incomplete until the side-effect
boundary is named. Inject a failure, then change producer and consumer contracts. The
trace distinguishes records in Kafka, committed progress, and business side effects.

{% interactive-block id="technology/kafka-delivery-semantics-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/kafka/data/delivery-failure-model.json" /%}

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Kafka transactions can atomically consume records, produce Kafka output, and commit
source offsets when all participants use the transaction and consumers read committed
data. They do not automatically include an unrelated database, email provider, or
payment API. Use an idempotency key, inbox/outbox pattern, or reconciliation at that
external boundary.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Make consumer progress match completed work

A consumer repeatedly polls assigned partitions, processes records, and commits an
offset representing the next record to read. Rebalances move partition ownership when
group membership or subscribed partitions change. Slow handlers can therefore create
lag, missed poll deadlines, and repeated work.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Poll a bounded batch" label="Fetch" %}
Keep the batch small enough to finish inside the poll interval, or move long work to a
bounded worker pool while preserving per-partition completion order.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Validate the event" label="Contract" %}
Reject malformed or unsupported schemas deliberately. Preserve the original record and
failure reason for quarantine instead of retrying permanent errors forever.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Apply idempotently" label="Side effect" %}
Use a stable event or operation ID at the destination. A replay should return the first
result or become a no-op rather than charge, email, or mutate twice.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Commit completed offsets" label="Progress" %}
Advance progress only after the owned side effect is durable. Track completion per
partition when work runs concurrently.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="5" title="Bound poison records" label="Recovery" %}
Retry transient failures with backoff and jitter, then quarantine after a finite
attempt budget. Alert on quarantine volume and age.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/kafka/code/process-order-events.py" language="python" title="An idempotent consumer boundary" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Choose retention and compaction for the recovery job

Kafka retains records independently of whether a consumer read them. The cleanup
policy should match why the history exists.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Delete retention" eyebrow="Time or size window" icon="clock" tone="blue" %}
Keep every record until old log segments cross the configured time or size boundary.
Use it for replay windows, event history, and bounded recovery.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Log compaction" eyebrow="Latest value per key" icon="archive" tone="violet" %}
Retain the latest known value for each key over time. Compaction is asynchronous and
does not turn Kafka into an immediate key-value lookup service.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Delete + compact" eyebrow="State plus bounded history" icon="layers" tone="green" %}
Apply both policies when consumers need recent event history and a compacted latest
state beyond that window. Tombstone and compaction timing still require planning.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Treat schemas as long-lived contracts

- Add fields with compatible defaults when older consumers must keep reading.
- Do not silently change a field's meaning, unit, or identity semantics.
- Version breaking contracts with an explicit migration and dual-read or dual-write
  window.
- Keep personally identifiable or secret data out of broad event streams unless the
  retention, deletion, encryption, and access model is intentional.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Design for failures that preserve the log but break the application

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Broker or disk failure" eyebrow="Replica loss" icon="server" tone="rose" %}
Leadership moves only to an eligible replica. Alert on under-replicated partitions,
offline partitions, shrinking ISR, disk errors, and recovery traffic.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Consumer lag" eyebrow="Work exceeds capacity" icon="gauge" tone="amber" %}
Lag can grow because of slow dependencies, partition skew, poison records, rebalances,
or insufficient consumers. Measure lag age as well as offset count.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Rebalance churn" eyebrow="Ownership keeps moving" icon="repeat" tone="violet" %}
Unstable membership pauses useful work and can repeat in-flight processing. Use stable
member identity where appropriate and keep poll behavior healthy.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Schema poison" eyebrow="Permanent incompatibility" icon="warning" tone="blue" %}
A deterministic parse error will not heal through retries. Quarantine it with context,
protect later records from starvation, and repair through a controlled replay.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Monitor the user-visible contract

- Produce request latency, error rate, timeouts, and throttling.
- Consumer lag by partition, lag age, processing latency, and retry or quarantine rate.
- Under-replicated and offline partitions, ISR changes, controller health, and leader
  imbalance.
- Disk usage, retention headroom, network saturation, page-cache pressure, and
  replication recovery load.
- End-to-end event age from source timestamp to completed business effect.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Secure and operate Kafka as shared infrastructure

Kafka often connects many trust domains, so a permissive cluster can become a broad
data-exfiltration and lateral-movement path.

- Encrypt client and inter-broker traffic with TLS and rotate credentials without a
  cluster-wide outage.
- Authenticate producers, consumers, brokers, and operators with a managed mechanism
  appropriate to the environment.
- Grant topic, group, transactional ID, and cluster permissions by workload identity;
  avoid one credential shared by unrelated services.
- Apply quotas and record-size limits so one producer or consumer cannot exhaust broker
  network, memory, request threads, or disk.
- Separate sensitive workloads or tenants when authorization alone cannot provide the
  required blast-radius and compliance boundary.
- Test broker loss, rolling upgrades, consumer rebalances, replay, and restore of
  dependent schema and connector services before an incident.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Know when Kafka is the wrong primitive

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Strong fit" eyebrow="Replayable fan-out" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Use Kafka for durable event distribution, change-data streams, event-driven integration,
stream processing inputs, and high-throughput pipelines with independent consumers.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Use a task queue" eyebrow="Per-message work dispatch" icon="route" tone="blue" %}
A conventional queue can be simpler when one worker should own each task, priority and
per-message routing dominate, and retained replay is not a requirement.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Use a database or object store" eyebrow="Query or archive" icon="database" tone="amber" %}
Kafka is not a replacement for transactional queries, arbitrary historical analytics,
large object storage, or a permanent backup. Sink durable history into the system built
for that access pattern.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

The operational cost is real: partition planning, schema governance, security,
connectors, capacity, upgrades, and incident response need explicit ownership. Adopt
Kafka when replayable streams and independent consumers justify that control plane.
{% /section-card %}

